    "body": "Fixes #2462.\n\n`IncrEval.reuse_check` could hand back a cached value that was a bare `Fun` \u2014 no `Closure`, so no environment. Applying it then lands in `Transition`'s\n\n```reason\n| FunNoEnv(dp, d3) when mode == `Substitution => \u2026step\u2026\n| FunNoEnv(_) => Indet          // environment mode: silently final\n```\n\nand the application is final-but-stuck forever. The issue has the full mechanism and why it presents nondeterministically in a live session; the short version is that `Transition`'s `Closure` rule evaluates subterms with `~in_closure`, which correctly omits the `Closure` wrapper for a function value nested under one \u2014 but a cache entry is keyed by id alone and is replayed at top level, where that enclosing `Closure` is gone.\n\nThe fix is a `carries_env` guard: don't reuse a value that carries no environment of its own.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe bug is a good example of a class of defect our evaluator tests structurally could not reach, so the test is worth a look.\n\n**Why a normal evaluator test can't see it.** Reuse is keyed on ids, so the cached ids have to survive from one program into the next. That only happens when the *same document* is edited in place. Two separate parses of the same text mint disjoint ids, so nothing is reused and nothing is exercised. Any reuse bug of this shape is invisible to a test that calls `Evaluator.evaluate` on parsed terms, however the terms are built.\n\n**The test runs the editor's real update loop**, one frame of what `ScratchMode` does:\n\n```reason\nCellEditor.Update.update(~settings, MainEditor(Perform(action)), model).model\n|> CellEditor.Update.calculate(\n     ~settings=settings.core, ~is_edited=true, ~queue_worker=None, ~stitch=x => x)\n```\n\nWith `~queue_worker=None`, `EvalResult.Update.calculate` builds the request itself, evaluates it through `WorkerServer.evaluate_sync`, and stores `incr_eval` in the model. So request construction, the choice of `EvalInfo.of_info_map`, and the threading of `prev` across keystrokes are all **covered** rather than restated by the test, and evaluation happens after every keystroke because that is what the editor does. Settings come from `Settings.Model.init`, the app's own defaults, rather than a hand-built `CoreSettings`.\n\nThat property is the point. An earlier version of this test built the request itself, and the first version of it reached for `EvalInfo.of_targets` \u2014 which leaves `reuse_check`'s `EvalInfo.find_opt` empty, so **incremental reuse never fires and the test passes no matter what `reuse_check` does**. A harness that restates the web stack can silently stop testing the thing it was written for; one that calls into it cannot. Worth knowing for any future evaluator harness.\n\nWhat's left in the test file is fixture setup (`Parser.to_zipper` into a `CellEditor.Model.t`), a two-line action wrapper, and reading the value out of `model.result`.\n\n**Determinism.** In a live session `prev` is a function of the whole editing history, so the same final text reproduces or not depending on how it was reached. The `test` block in the fixture pins that ingredient down so the regression test doesn't depend on history \u2014 see the comment above it.\n\nIt lives in `test/Test_CellEditor.re`, since `CellEditor` is the module it drives.\n\n## Verification\n\n- The new test fails on `dev` and passes with the guard.\n- 460 `Evaluator` tests pass, including all incremental-reuse tests.\n- `make` clean, no new warnings.\n- Also confirmed end-to-end in the browser on the branch where this was found.\n\n## Follow-up, not in this PR\n\n`| FunNoEnv(_) => Indet` fails silently \u2014 no error, no warning, just an application that stops reducing. A `DynamicErrorHole` there would have made this a much faster diagnosis. Noted on #2462.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
